# Service-owned PARC catalog

Keep a reviewed manifest in your service's source. It contains `service`, a
consecutive integer `version`, `permissions`, and `roles`. Each permission has
`key` (`<service>.<action>`), `action`, `description`, `surface` and concrete
Service Access `capabilities`. Each role has `key`, `description` and an explicit
subset of the permission keys. Define read-only/operator/admin templates from
actual routes; do not infer grants from names or use wildcards. Administrative
`access.*` and `permission-catalog.*` permissions cannot be self-published.

Call `new CatalogPublisher(parcHttpsOrigin, serviceKey).publish(manifest,
{ lease, credential })` from a separately admitted publication path. Obtain a
fresh, correctly scoped Service Access lease for `<service>.permission-catalog.publish`.
The publisher does not issue, inspect, consume or log credentials. PARC consumes
the lease. Do not reuse a request's already consumed lease or auto-publish on
application startup. Do not use browser tokens or a shared API key.

PARC owns canonical validation and persistence. Identical retries of the current
version are idempotent, but require fresh authority. Transport errors can follow
a successful commit: reconcile with PARC before advancing the version. The
client never retries automatically and verifies the acknowledgement's exact
service, version and canonical digest. Catalog publication never assigns roles.
Existing role assignments retain their selected catalog version; adoption is a
separate authorized operation. Compliance and business invariants still belong
to their domain services.

The recipe does not edit existing authorization recipes, application startup,
configuration, manifests or package dependencies. It rejects differing generated
files and symlink targets; identical regeneration is safe. PARC's catalog API
must be deployed and enabled through its release gates before publication.
